<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Amangkurat II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1703">1703</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Amangkurat II was ruler of Mataram from 1677 to 1703, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-i">Amangkurat I</a>. To defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya-rebellion">Trunajaya rebellion</a> he allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>, granting it ports, trade monopolies and payment of its costs. With Company troops he crushed the rebels by 1680 and founded a new capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartasura">Kartasura</a>, but the alliance left Mataram dependent on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/amangkurat-ii.jpg" alt="Amangkurat II" width="320" height="300" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tirto (maker, from Gresik) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Amangkurat_II_(De_moord_op_kapitein_Tack_in_Kartasura).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Born Raden Mas Rahmat, he was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-i">Amangkurat I</a> and Ratu Kulon, daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pangeran-pekik">Pangeran Pekik</a> of Surabaya. Raised in Surabaya, he moved to the Plered Palace as crown prince but quarrelled with his brother Prince Singasari and then with his father. He rebelled in 1661, and in 1663 his father tried and failed to poison him. In 1668 Pangeran Pekik took Rara Oyi, a girl meant for Amangkurat I, to marry Rahmat; the king killed Pekik and his family, and pardoned Rahmat only after forcing him to kill Rara Oyi.</p><p>Stripped of his rank in favour of Prince Puger, Rahmat turned in 1670 to the spiritual teacher Panembahan Rama of the Kajoran family, who introduced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madura">Madura</a>. When Trunajaya's revolt in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> grew beyond control, Rahmat went back to his father and regained the title of crown prince. On 2 July 1677 Trunajaya took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plered">Plered</a>, and father and son fled west.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-i">Amangkurat I</a> died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tegal">Tegal</a>, poisoned by his son according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babad-tanah-jawi">Babad Tanah Jawi</a>. Without an army or treasury, Amangkurat II bought the help of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-east-india-company">Dutch East India Company</a>: by the treaty signed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jepara">Jepara</a> in September 1677 with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cornelis-speelman">Cornelis Speelman</a>, most of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>'s north coast was mortgaged to the Company against the cost of the war. Company forces defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trunajaya">Trunajaya</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kediri-kingdom">Kediri</a> in November 1678, and the king killed the captured rebel with his own hands on 2 January 1680. Giri Kedaton, the last ally of the rebels with a large army, was destroyed in 1680, and Prince Puger, who had claimed the throne, surrendered on 28 November 1681.</p><p>Burdened by a war debt of 2.5 million guilders, the king resented growing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> control of the coast and worked against the Company by intrigue. He sheltered Untung Surapati, who was wanted by the Dutch; Captain Francois Tack was killed pursuing Surapati at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kartasura">Kartasura</a> in 1685. Letters later found showed the king urging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirebon">Cirebon</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johor">Johor</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> to war against the Dutch, and he supported Captain Jonker's revolt in 1689.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Amangkurat II was the first Javanese monarch to wear a European-style uniform, which earned him the name Sunan Amral, from the word for admiral. Javanese chronicles portray him as weak and easily swayed. He died on 3 November 1703 and was succeeded by his only child, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amangkurat-iii">Amangkurat III</a>, whose short reign saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-javanese-war-of-succession">First Javanese War of Succession</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Amangkurat II of Mataram” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His son <b>Amangkurat II</b> secured the help of the Dutch East India Company in exchange for ports, revenues and trade monopolies, defeated Trunajaya by 1680 and built a new capital at Kartasura.</blockquote><a href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
